@388[12]. "The Assyrian" (Ezekiel 31) = the Rational; and "a cedar," the Intellectual.

@405[15]. "The cedars of God" (Psalms 80:10) = spiritual truths.

@--[29]. "Lebanon" (Isaiah 37:24) = the spiritual Church; and "the cedars" = its internal truths which are from good...

@410[3]. By "the cedars of Lebanon, and the oaks of Bashan" (Isaiah 2:13) is signified conceit from man's Own intelligence; interior conceit, by "the cedars of Lebanon;" and exterior, by "the oaks of Bashan."

@458[6]. "Palm" (Psalms 92) = spiritual good; "cedar," the truth of that good; and "Lebanon" the spiritual Church.

@514[9]. "Cedars," in the Word, are predicated of the rational man; and "oaks," of the natural.

@518[13]. "Lign aloes" (Numbers 24:6) = those things which are of the natural man; "cedars," those things which are of the rational man.

@638[23]. "The cedars of Lebanon" (Judges 9) = the rational things which are from truths.

@650[57]. "The cedar which He has made bare" = the Rational destroyed.

@654[13]. "A cedar" (Ezekiel 31) = the Rational of the man of the Church which is from a spiritual origin.

@730[24]. "The cedar" (of Shittah) = higher rational truth; "the myrtle," lower rational truth.

@1029[13]. "Oaks, and cedars of Lebanon" (Isaiah 14:8) = Knowledges of good and truth in the external sense, and in the internal.

@1100[5]. "A cedar" here (Ezekiel 17), as elsewhere in the Word = the spiritual rational Church, such as the Church with the ancients after the flood.

@1144[2]. Wood from... the cedar = rational good.

